From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: rockchip: grf: rk3128: give the VPU/HEVC shared bus to
 the VPU

The RK3128 VPU1 (Hantro G1/H1, 0x10106000) and the HEVC decoder
(0x10104000) share one bus interface. GRF_SOC_CON1 bit 15 selects the
owner: 0 = VPU, 1 = HEVC (the vendor vcodec_service's "vpu_combo",
mode_bit 15, mode_ctrl 0x144, flips it before every job). The Hantro
driver never touches it, so whatever the previous owner of the SoC left
behind decides whether the decoder answers.

Set it to the VPU at GRF init, as is already done for the VPU main clock
select next to it. A later HEVC driver that arbitrates the mux flips it
at run time; this only fixes the boot state.

Signed-off-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/soc/rockchip/grf.c b/drivers/soc/rockchip/grf.c
index b459607c1..e2ad6d0e3 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/rockchip/grf.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/rockchip/grf.c
@@ -45,6 +45,13 @@ static const struct rockchip_grf_info rk3036_grf __initconst = {
 static const struct rockchip_grf_value rk3128_defaults[] __initconst = {
 	{ "jtag switching", RK3128_GRF_SOC_CON0, FIELD_PREP_WM16_CONST(BIT(8), 0) },
 	{ "vpu main clock", RK3128_GRF_SOC_CON1, FIELD_PREP_WM16_CONST(BIT(10), 0) },
+	/*
+	 * The VPU1 (Hantro G1/H1) and the HEVC decoder share one bus interface, switched by
+	 * this bit: 0 gives it to the VPU, 1 to the HEVC block. The bootloader or the previous
+	 * system may have left it on HEVC (the vendor kernel flips it before every job), so
+	 * make the Hantro driver's block the default owner.
+	 */
+	{ "vpu/hevc bus mux: vpu", RK3128_GRF_SOC_CON1, FIELD_PREP_WM16_CONST(BIT(15), 0) },
 };
 
 static const struct rockchip_grf_info rk3128_grf __initconst = {
